scope: |
  A TUPLE HAS NO TYPE HEAD in `rewriteGivenExtensionCalls`, so `Bifunctor[Tuple2]` can never be
  found. Found while scoping SSC3-15, and it is NOT that debt — it is a defect in the approximation
  SSC3-15 defers replacing.

  THE MEASUREMENT THAT REDIRECTED ME, and it killed my first hypothesis rather than confirming it.
  `tests/conformance/std-bifunctor.ssc:19` refuses `t.bimap(…)` where `t` is
  `val t: (Int, String) = (10, "ok")`. The obvious reading is §4a1's first bullet — "`Stmt.Val`
  records NO declared type" — so I was about to make `Stmt.Val` carry the type the author WROTE,
  which is not inference and looked like the honest first slice. Probed first:

      def go(t: (Int, String)) = t.bimap(…)        -> STILL REFUSED   (declared type, a PARAMETER)
      (10, "ok").bimap(…)                          -> STILL REFUSED   (constructor receiver)
      def go(e: Either[String, Int]) = e.bimap(…)  -> Right(43)       WORKS

  So the declared-type mechanism is fine and the val's missing type is not the blocker here. Both
  tuple paths fail for one reason: nothing maps a tuple to the head `Tuple2`.

  TWO SITES, both in `rewriteGivenExtensionCalls`:
    - `typeOfCtor` knows five built-in constructors and declared classes. A tuple literal is
      `Expr.Call("Tuple2", …)` — checked with `ssc3 ast` — and `Tuple2` is neither, so the
      constructor source yields nothing.
    - the `params` head map uses `headAndArg`, which splits on `[`. `(Int,String)` has no `[`, so
      the head comes out as the whole text and matches no instance.

  NARROW: teach both that `TupleN` names its own type and that a parenthesised type text of arity N
  is `TupleN`. No new resolution SOURCE, no `Stmt.Val` change, nothing from the type-checker debt.

  EXPECTED YIELD, said before the edit: `std-bifunctor` and nothing else I have found —
  `std-monaderror` fails on a different shape (a method on a named given INSTANCE) and is not
  touched here. So N 216 -> 217 at most; DIFF 3 and CRASH 9 are floors.
next: map TupleN in both sites, probe the three shapes above, then measure N
